# Spec (rule) docs — consumer template

Copy this directory into your repo as `docs/spec/`. It is the **reflect/specify** lane of the
spec-driven loop: write each rule down once, in the mini-language, so a test (and — for an
order/concurrency rule — a model assert) can cite it by ID. See the gatecrate
[`spec-rules`](https://github.com/Yos-K/gatecrate/blob/main/docs/spec-rules.md) guide for the full
rationale. Replace this index and `area.md` with your project's areas.

## Mini-language

| Form | Meaning | Example |
|---|---|---|
| `R-n invariant "..."` | a condition that must always hold | `R-1 invariant "title is 1..200 chars"` |
| `R-n policy "..." when <Event>` | an automatic consequence of an event | `R-7 policy "re-list after a successful mutation" when MutationSucceeded` |
| `R-n error <Name> "..."` | a named failure / forbidden state | `R-12 error Corrupted "stored data is malformed"` |

Write the description in domain words, not code. The ID is the reference point for the test.

## 1 rule = 2 reflections

| Reflection | Role | When |
|---|---|---|
| implementation **test** (example / PBT) | rejection signal on real code, every PR in CI | every rule |
| model **assert** (Alloy / TLA+) | exhaustive state-space check of operation orderings | **order/concurrency rules only** |

Pure input-space invariants need only the test. If you have no model tooling, mark an order rule
`model: candidate`.

## intent/defect gate (the one human step)

A rule derived from how the code *currently behaves* must be classified by a human as **intent**
(matches the spec/requirements) or **defect** (a bug to fix) before it is canonized — otherwise a
test freezes a bug as spec. Mark fresh rules `R-? (draft, needs intent/defect classification)`.

## Keeping it honest (rule-doc currency)

Wire `check-rule-doc-currency.sh` so these docs can't drift from the code: copy
`rule-doc-lanes.tsv.example` to your repo root as `rule-doc-lanes.tsv`, map each rule-bearing source
file to its `docs/spec/<area>.md`, and run the gate in CI (a PR that edits a rule-bearing file
without updating its spec then fails; a pure refactor declares `Docs-Impact: none (...)`).

